CloudBurrow patch 75 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1599).

ABS converted its argument to float64, so the ABS of an INT64 above 2^53
lost its low bits (ABS(-9007199254740993) was 9007199254740992), and of
the INT64 minimum gave 2^63 rounded, not BigQuery's error; SAFE_ADD,
SAFE_SUBTRACT, SAFE_MULTIPLY and SAFE_NEGATE did the same. INT64 +, - and
* wrapped around on overflow. And the safe form of add, subtract,
multiply and divide (googlesqlite_safe_multiply) has the name of
SAFE_ADD, SAFE_SUBTRACT, SAFE_MULTIPLY and SAFE_DIVIDE, so which of the
two SQLite kept hung on a Go map's order: SAFE_MULTIPLY(-2^63, -1) gave
the wrapped -2^63, not NULL.

Now ABS of an INT64 is exact, and of -9223372036854775808 fails
"int64 overflow: ABS(-9223372036854775808)" (the form of unary minus's
error, patch 0005); ABS of a NUMERIC or BIGNUMERIC is exact; the SAFE_
functions of INT64s are exact, and NULL where the result overflows
INT64, as BigQuery's mathematical functions reference documents; INT64
+, - and * fail "int64 overflow: 9223372036854775807 + 1" (GoogleSQL's
form); and a function's own name wins over another's safe form.

